# {{ name }} — Docker build context exclusions.
#
# The generated Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, and its context is the resolved
# tracing root — which is NOT always this directory. When the lockfile walk
# finds a marker above the app, the context widens to that ancestor, and
# everything under it is uploaded to the Docker daemon and baked into the
# builder stage.
#
# `kn-next create` warns when it detects more than one root marker. This file is
# what makes a wrong context HARMLESS rather than merely reported.
#
# Ordered by what it costs to get wrong: secrets first, then size.

# ── Secrets and local credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────
# A `.env` in the context ends up in the builder layer. Layers are extractable
# from any image you push, so this is not "someone would have to exec in" — it
# is readable by anyone who can pull. The runtime reads config from Kubernetes
# Secrets via env (see `.claude/rules/security.md`), never from a file in the
# image, so nothing here is needed at build time.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
.npmrc
.netrc
kubeconfig
.kube/

# ── Version control and CI ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.git` is frequently the largest single directory in a repo and carries the
# full history — including anything ever committed and later removed.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.gitlab-ci.yml

# ── Dependencies and build output ────────────────────────────────────────────
# All regenerated inside the image. Copying a host `node_modules` is also a
# correctness hazard: native modules built for the host libc do not run on the
# runtime base image.
node_modules
.next
.output
.vinext
dist
build
*.tsbuildinfo

# Compiled single-executables are ~60-90 MB each and are build OUTPUT.
knext-exec*
server

# ── Test, coverage and editor noise ──────────────────────────────────────────
coverage
.nyc_output
*.log
.DS_Store
.idea
.vscode
*.swp

# ── Docker's own files ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing in the image needs them, and excluding them keeps an edit to the
# Dockerfile from invalidating the COPY layer's cache.
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
